To understand decoding algorithms like **Minimum-Weight Perfect Matching (MWPM)** and **Union-Find (UF)**, we must examine the formal mathematical machinery of **Quantum Stabilizer Codes**, **Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) estimation**, **Integer Linear Programming (ILP)**, and **Homology**.

---

## 1. The Quantum Stabilizer Formalism & Error Model

### Linear Algebra over $\mathbb{F}_2$

Let $\mathcal{H} = (\mathbb{C}^2)^{\otimes n}$ be the Hilbert space of $n$ physical qubits. The **$n$-qubit Pauli Group** $\mathcal{P}_n$ consists of tensor products of Pauli operators:

$$\mathcal{P}_n = \{ \pm 1, \pm i \} \times \{I, X, Y, Z\}^{\otimes n}$$

A **Stabilizer Code** $\mathcal{C}$ is defined by an $n-k$ generator abelian subgroup $\mathcal{S} \subset \mathcal{P}_n$ such that $-I \notin \mathcal{S}$. The code space $\mathcal{C}$ is the simultaneous $+1$-eigenspace of all operators in $\mathcal{S}$:

$$\mathcal{C} = \Big\{ \vert{}\psi\rangle \in \mathcal{H} \;:\; S_i \vert{}\psi\rangle = \vert{}\psi\rangle, \quad \forall S_i \in \mathcal{S} \Big\}$$

When an error $E \in \mathcal{P}_n$ occurs on state $\vert{}\psi\rangle \in \mathcal{C}$, measuring the stabilizer generators $\{S_1, S_2, \dots, S_m\}$ (where $m = n-k$) yields a binary **syndrome vector** $\mathbf{s} = (s_1, s_2, \dots, s_m)^T \in \mathbb{F}_2^m$:

$$s_i = \begin{cases} 0, & \text{if } [S_i, E] = 0 \quad \text{(Commute)} \\ 1, & \text{if } \{S_i, E\} = 0 \quad \text{(Anti-commute)} \end{cases}$$

### The Parity-Check Matrix

By mapping Pauli operators to binary vectors via $X^a Z^b \mapsto (a \mid b) \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$, we represent any error $E$ as a vector $\mathbf{e} \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$. The commutation relations yield a classical linear system over $\mathbb{F}_2$:

$$H \mathbf{e} \equiv \mathbf{s} \pmod 2$$

where $H \in \mathbb{F}_2^{m \times 2n}$ is the parity-check matrix of the quantum code.

---

## 2. Mathematical Formulation of the Decoding Problem

Given an observed syndrome $\mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{F}_2^m$, the decoder must output an estimated correction $\hat{\mathbf{e}} \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$ such that $H \hat{\mathbf{e}} = \mathbf{s}$.

### Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) Decoding

Assuming independent physical errors where qubit $j$ suffers an error with probability $p_j < 0.5$, the probability of error vector $\mathbf{e}$ is:

$$P(\mathbf{e}) = \prod_{j=1}^{2n} p_j^{e_j} (1 - p_j)^{1 - e_j}$$

The optimal MAP decoder maximizes the posterior probability:

$$\hat{\mathbf{e}} = \arg\max_{\mathbf{e} \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}: H\mathbf{e} = \mathbf{s}} P(\mathbf{e})$$

Taking the negative logarithm yields an equivalent weight minimization problem:

$$-\ln P(\mathbf{e}) = \sum_{j=1}^{2n} e_j \ln \left( \frac{1 - p_j}{p_j} \right) - \sum_{j=1}^{2n} \ln(1 - p_j)$$

Defining the edge weights $w_j = \ln \left( \frac{1 - p_j}{p_j} \right) > 0$, the MAP decoding problem maps to finding the **minimum-weight error error string**:

$$\hat{\mathbf{e}} = \arg\min_{\mathbf{e}: H\mathbf{e} = \mathbf{s}} \sum_{j=1}^{2n} w_j e_j$$

For identical error rates ($p_j = p$), this reduces to finding $\mathbf{e}$ that minimizes the **Hamming weight** $\vert{}\mathbf{e}\vert{} = \sum e_j$.

