## 3. Minimum-Weight Perfect Matching (MWPM) Mathematics

MWPM transforms the linear algebraic problem over $\mathbb{F}_2$ into a graph optimization problem.

### Graph Construction

Let $V_s = \{ v \in \{1,\dots,m\} : s_v = 1 \}$ be the set of defect vertices. We construct a complete graph $G_M = (V_M, E_M, w)$ where $V_M = V_s \cup V_{\text{boundary}}$.

The weight $w(u,v)$ between any two defect nodes $u, v \in V_M$ is defined by the shortest-path metric on the dual lattice:

$$w(u,v) = \min_{P \in \text{Paths}(u,v)} \sum_{j \in P} w_j$$

### Integer Linear Program (ILP)

Finding the optimal pairing of defects is formulated as a **0-1 Integer Linear Program**:

$$\min_{\mathbf{x}} \sum_{(u,v) \in E_M} w(u,v) x_{uv}$$

$$\text{subject to } \sum_{v \in V_M} x_{uv} = 1, \quad \forall u \in V_M \quad \text{(Perfect Matching Constraint)}$$

$$x_{uv} \in \{0, 1\}, \quad \forall (u,v) \in E_M$$

### LP Duality and Edmonds' Blossom Algorithm

Because integer linear programming is generally NP-hard, Edmonds' algorithm relaxes the integer constraint to $x_{uv} \ge 0$ and solves its **Dual Linear Program**:

$$\max_{\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{Y}} \sum_{u \in V_M} y_u + \sum_{B \in \mathcal{B}} Y_B$$

$$\text{subject to } y_u + y_v + \sum_{B : (u,v) \in \delta(B)} Y_B \le w(u,v), \quad \forall (u,v) \in E_M$$

where $y_u \in \mathbb{R}$ are vertex potentials, $\mathcal{B}$ is the set of all odd-cardinality subsets of $V_M$ ($\vert{}B\vert{} \ge 3$, called **blossoms**), $Y_B \ge 0$ are blossom potentials, and $\delta(B)$ is the edge cut of $B$.

* **Blossom Contraction:** When an odd cycle of tight constraints $y_u + y_v = w(u,v)$ forms, the algorithm contracts the set of nodes $B$ into a single pseudo-node $v_B$.
* This contraction prevents fractional solutions ($x_{uv} = 0.5$) and guarantees an exact integral solution in $O(\vert{}V_M\vert{}^3)$ time.

